---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Accessibility';
const description = 'World Battery Hub works toward WCAG 2.1 Level AA — our accessibility approach, known limitations, and how to report a barrier.';
const canonical = '/accessibility/';

const jsonLd = [{
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Accessibility',
  description,
  url: 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/accessibility/',
}];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  dateModified="2026-09-21"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Legal · Last reviewed 2026-09-21</p>
      <h1>Accessibility</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A reference platform is only useful if it can be read. World Battery Hub aims to keep its content usable
        by people with a wide range of abilities and with the assistive technology they use.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>At a glance:</strong> We work toward the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1
        Level AA for this site, we fix barriers when they are reported, and reporting one is easy — email
        <a href="mailto:editorial@worldbatteryhub.com">editorial@worldbatteryhub.com</a> with the page and what
        went wrong.
      </div>

      <h2>1. Our approach</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Semantic structure.</strong> Pages use headings in order, landmarks, lists and tables with
            header cells, so screen readers and keyboard navigation can move through a page logically.</li>
        <li><strong>Keyboard access.</strong> Navigation, links, form-free interactions and our calculators are
            operable without a mouse where they are interactive.</li>
        <li><strong>Text alternatives.</strong> Meaningful graphics and diagrams carry text alternatives; purely
            decorative elements are marked so assistive technology can skip them.</li>
        <li><strong>Readable presentation.</strong> Content is written in plain language with expandable
            definitions in the <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>, and pages are built to remain usable when text
            is enlarged or the layout reflows to a narrow screen.</li>
        <li><strong>Colour.</strong> Information is not conveyed by colour alone, and we aim for contrast
            between text and background that meets the AA threshold.</li>
        <li><strong>No motion traps.</strong> We do not use autoplaying media, and we do not rely on animation to
            convey information.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. Known limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dense data tables.</strong> Some specification and comparison tables are wide and
            information-dense. On small screens they may require horizontal scrolling; if a particular table is
            hard to use with your setup, tell us the page and we will look at an alternative presentation.</li>
        <li><strong>Calculator tools.</strong> Our calculators are designed to be keyboard-operable and to
            announce their results, but their usability with every assistive-technology combination has not been
            exhaustively tested. Feedback here is especially useful.</li>
        <li><strong>Third-party content.</strong> External sites we link to are outside our control and may not
            meet the same standard.</li>
        <li><strong>Older pages.</strong> Pages written before this statement may not yet fully meet it; they are
            brought into line as they are reviewed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Report an accessibility barrier</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href="mailto:editorial@worldbatteryhub.com">editorial@worldbatteryhub.com</a> and include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the page address (URL);</li>
        <li>what you were trying to do;</li>
        <li>what happened instead — including any error, or the assistive technology, browser and device you
            were using;</li>
        <li>your preferred way to receive a reply (for example, a description by email rather than a link).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We treat accessibility reports as editorial issues: we aim to acknowledge them and to fix or work around
        a barrier as part of our normal review cycle. If we cannot fix it quickly, we will tell you what we can
        do in the meantime — for example by providing the information in another form.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Standards reference</h2>
      <p>
        We use the <strong>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1, Level AA</strong> as our working
        target. This page is a statement of intent and practice, not a certification, and it does not claim
        conformance for every page at every moment.
      </p>

      <h2>Related policies</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/terms-of-use/">Terms of Use</a></li>
        <li><a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a></li>
        <li><a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a></li>
        <li><a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
